Why the split
--------------
- The frontend and SDK are open immediately so anyone can read, audit, run,
  and build against Swipe.
- The contracts are source-available and auditable on day one, but BUSL-1.1
  stops a verbatim fork from being redeployed as a competing product before
  the protocol has had time to establish itself — the same model used by
  Uniswap v3 and Aave v3. The license converts to Apache 2.0 on the Change
  Date above, after which the contracts become fully permissive.
- The AI assistant is the one piece of the stack that is a genuine product
  differentiator today, so it stays closed for now. This may change later;
  it is not a permanent design decision.

Questions about any of the above, or about commercial licensing of the
contracts ahead of the Change Date, should be opened as an issue at
